/**
 * Day 93 — Phase 5 country pack 6, South Asia: Pakistan (ROADMAP item 6, first country).
 *
 * Before this, the Indus lands between the Kushans and the Sikh Empire were drawn only where
 * empires based elsewhere reached them: the Kabul Shahis, Ghaznavids and Ghurids in the Punjab
 * (Afghanistan pack), the Mongol steppe ring, the Timurids, Mughal Kabul and the Durrani Empire,
 * and the edges of the schematic Maurya, Gupta, Delhi and Mughal 'india-north' templates. Nothing
 * drew the Indus Valley Civilisation, the kingdoms and emirates of Sindh and Multan, the Soomras,
 * Sammas, Langahs, Arghuns, Kalhoras and Talpurs, the Mughal subahs of Lahore, Multan and Thatta,
 * the Sikh misls or the Khanate of Kalat. This pack adds 18 polities from 2600 BCE to 1813. The
 * nations layer draws Pakistan (id `pakistan`), Talpur Sindh (`sindh`, 1815–1843) and Kalat
 * (`kalat`, from 1815); this pack uses other ids and has no keyframe after 1813.
 *
 * How it is built (docs/DATA.md, "Country-pack globe backfill"):
 *  - 10 new pieces from Natural Earth 1:10m admin-1 regions (public domain), simplified as one
 *    coverage and clipped to the globe’s land: Sindh cut at 26.55°N (upper Sindh: Aror, Bhakkar,
 *    Mohenjo-daro | lower Sindh: Sehwan, Thatta, Hyderabad, Karachi); Balochistan cut along
 *    27.15°N and 65.45°E (Makran | Kalat and the Brahui and Baloch highlands); the Day 77 PUNJAB
 *    piece split along a straight line from (68.8, 31.67) to (75.2, 29.33) into the northern
 *    Punjab (Lahore, Harappa, the Salt Range) and Multan (Multan, Uch, Bahawalpur); and, for the
 *    Harappan extent, India’s Punjab, Haryana with Delhi, north-west Rajasthan (north of 27.9°N:
 *    Kalibangan) and western Gujarat (west of 72.8°E: Kutch, Saurashtra, Lothal). They are noded
 *    with the Day 77 PUNJAB, PESHAWAR, KASHMIR, Kandahar, Sistan and Ghazni pieces, so shared
 *    edges carry the same vertices. Generator: et-tools day93-pieces.py (outside the repo).
 *  - A polity is a dated list of pieces; every change is a one-year step, and a polity that
 *    loses everything and comes back (the Mughals in the Sur years) is two shapes.
 *  - Every polity has a certainty level and sources, and links the Pakistan timeline items.
 *
 * Not drawn (timeline items and pins only): the Achaemenid, Alexander, Mauryan, Indo-Greek,
 * Indo-Scythian, Indo-Parthian and Kushan north-west beyond the existing shapes; the Alchon Huns;
 * Lahore and the northern Punjab between the Ghurids and the Delhi conquest (1206–1228) and
 * between Timur and the Mughals (1398–1525); Durrani Multan, Peshawar and Derajat (1752–1818),
 * Bahawalpur (1748), Chitral, Gilgit and Baltistan; Quetta’s and Sibi’s changes of hands; and
 * Harappan sites in Balochistan and Makran (Nausharo, Sutkagan Dor).
 */
